d values. This is considered one of the worst starting hands in poker.\n\n**Position & Action:** My opponent checked (action: 0), meaning they just called the big blind. I'm facing a decision with minimal investment so far.\n\n**Possible Moves:**\n1. **Fold (-1):** Given the extremely weak hand, folding is the safest option. 6-4 offsuit has very poor equity against most hands and rarely makes strong hands.\n\n2. **Check (0):** This allows me to see the flop cheaply. With only 100 chips invested (the big blind), I could see if the flop miraculously helps me. However, even if I hit a pair, it will likely be bottom pair with a weak kicker.\n\n3. **Raise (200+):** Raising with this hand would be a pure bluff. While bluffing has merit in poker, doing it with 6-4 offsuit is extremely risky because:\n   - If called, I have almost no equity\n   - If re-raised, I must fold and lose chips\n   - It's the first hand; establishing a overly aggressive image early isn't necessary\n\n**Risk Assessment:** \n- I'm already down 100 chips this hand\n- This is hand 1 of 20, so there's plenty of time to find better spots\n- My opponent checked, suggesting they might not have a premium hand either, but almost any two cards have better equity than 6-4 offsuit\n\nReason:\nWhile checking seems tempting to see a cheap flop, 6-4 offsuit is one of the absolute worst starting hands in Texas Hold'em. Even if I see the flop for free, the likelihood of making a strong hand is extremely low, and I'll often be in difficult spots on later streets. The disciplined play is to fold and wait for better opportunities in the remaining 19 hands. Saving chips for stronger starting hands is the optimal long-term strategy.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
